### 1. Task outcome (short version):
In HIV-infected Zambian children, daily cotrimoxazole prophylaxis significantly reduced all-cause mortality compared 
with placebo (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002) over a median 19-month follow-up, with no increase in overall grade 
3/4 adverse events.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- Population: Children 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection in Zambia (541 randomized; 7 later 
HIV-negative excluded). Double-blind RCT in a high co-trimoxazole resistance setting.
- Intervention: Daily cotrimoxazole (trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole) prophylaxis; dose not specified in abstract.
- Comparator: Daily placebo; double-blind.
- Follow-up: Median 19 months; subgroup beyond 12 months (n=320).
- Outcomes (mortality and illness episodes):
  • All-cause mortality ITT: 74 deaths (co-trimoxazole) vs 112 (placebo); HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77), p=0.0002.
  • Mortality among >12-month follow-up: HR 0.48 (95% CI 0.27–0.84); heterogeneity by follow-up duration p=0.60.
  • Consistency: No heterogeneity by age (p=0.82) or baseline CD4 count (p=0.36).
  • PCP: P. carinii detected in 1/73 nasopharyngeal aspirates from pneumonia cases (placebo group); events rare; no 
comparative rate reported.
- Adverse events and safety: Grade 3/4 AEs 6% (co-trimoxazole) vs 7% (placebo). Severe neutropenia on one occasion 6% vs
3% (p=0.06). One rash in placebo reported.
- Antimicrobial resistance outcomes: Not measured/reported; study conducted in setting with high background resistance.
- Interpretation: Routine cotrimoxazole prophylaxis reduced mortality across ages and CD4 strata without increasing 
serious AEs overall.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
The abstract does not specify dosing or provide per-arm denominators beyond death counts, and does not report on 
bacterial infections, malaria, or hospitalizations as outcomes. Author/year were not included in the abstract; based on 
the CHAP trial, this is likely Chintu et al., 2004 (Lancet), but this should be verified against the full text.
